# Fixed 2026-09-30, defect 135's class, lane recovery-b3 (lane recovery-b2's
# report, M1). Symptom: `record Pair<T>` over its fields cost
# `continuation_outside_brackets`, the line said to be one that cannot end
# with `>`, and `expected_end_of_line`, and the certain join wrote the first
# field into the head, `record Pair<T> a: T`, refused anew; `variant Shape<T,
# U>` cost four messages and the join `variant Shape<T, U> one`; and `record
# Braced<T> {` cost `empty_record` and `expected_declaration` at its `}`.
# Cause: the lexer read the `>` as a comparison no line may end with, and so
# did the brace habit before a `{`. Now a record's or a variant's head that
# ends with the `>` of type parameters ends there
# (`line_above.closes_type_parameters`), the parser tells the `<` once,
# saying type parameters are a function's alone (spec § 9), and the members
# below it, or in braces, are read: their own mistakes are told.

record Pair<T>  #~ expected_end_of_line
    a: T
    b i64  #~ expected_field_type

record Box<T>  #~ expected_end_of_line
    v: T

record Nothing<>  #~ expected_end_of_line
    v: i64

variant Shape<T, U>  #~ expected_end_of_line
    one
        v: T
    two
        w: U

record Braced<T> {  #~ expected_end_of_line empty_record
    a: T
}

function main()
    print(1)

variant Opt<T>  #~ expected_end_of_line
    none
    some
        v: T
